WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:10.471
John pulled the diving mask over his face and gave the boat's captain the thumbs-up.

2
00:00:11.011 --> 00:00:16.833
He held on tightly to his underwater camera, leaned back, and let his own weight carry him overboard.

3
00:00:17.473 --> 00:00:23.274
He began to descend to one of the earth's greatest natural wonders: the Great Australian Barrier Reef.

4
00:00:25.475 --> 00:00:29.016
Slowly John's eyes adjusted to the reduced level of light underwater.

5
00:00:29.672 --> 00:00:31.273
He was amazed by what he saw.

6
00:00:31.913 --> 00:00:34.354
Large groups of brightly colored fish swam past.

7
00:00:35.034 --> 00:00:39.056
One group was a brilliant yellow; the next striped with orange and black lines.

8
00:00:39.796 --> 00:00:43.057
Each group was like a wonderful moving painting of amazing colors.

9
00:00:45.218 --> 00:00:51.801
Everywhere he looked, he saw a different kind of marvelous fish, a different expression of nature's beauty.

10
00:00:52.621 --> 00:00:54.002
Then the reef came into view.

11
00:00:54.522 --> 00:00:56.203
It looked like an underwater city.

12
00:00:56.843 --> 00:00:59.304
Most of the reef seemed to be covered with green algae.

13
00:00:59.924 --> 00:01:05.487
All around, however, the colorful animals and plants that clung to and swam near the coral reef stood

14
00:01:05.568 --> 00:01:06.888
out in sharp contrast.

15
00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:11.311
Blue starfish crawled slowly over the reef.

16
00:01:12.212 --> 00:01:17.375
Strange multicolored fish called flatworms performed an underwater ballet as they swam above it.

17
00:01:17.995 --> 00:01:23.538
Overwhelmed by the beauty of the reef, minutes passed before John remembered that he had his camera; then,

18
00:01:23.698 --> 00:01:25.540
quickly, he began to take pictures.

19
00:01:27.972 --> 00:01:33.294
Australia's coral reef, at two thousand kilometers long, is the largest in the world and the one most

20
00:01:33.394 --> 00:01:34.435
visited by divers.

21
00:01:35.255 --> 00:01:37.296
But coral reefs are not well-understood.

22
00:01:38.056 --> 00:01:40.017
Many believe coral is a type of plant.

23
00:01:40.877 --> 00:01:41.377
It's not.

24
00:01:41.878 --> 00:01:44.959
Corals are small animals that eat the tiniest shellfish.

25
00:01:45.579 --> 00:01:51.061
Most corals swim through the ocean, but others called hard corals make a permanent home for themselves.

26
00:01:53.822 --> 00:01:57.524
Hard corals take carbon and phosphates out of the water to make limestone.

27
00:01:58.332 --> 00:02:02.416
As the limestone structure where they live builds up, it becomes a coral reef.

28
00:02:03.076 --> 00:02:09.041
Each individual coral is known as a coral polyp and can be as small as five millimeters across.

29
00:02:09.842 --> 00:02:13.945
It takes millions of coral polyps, living side by side, to make a coral reef.

30
00:02:16.267 --> 00:02:21.672
Each generation of hard corals builds on the remains of its ancestors, and over time, the reefs grow.

31
00:02:22.484 --> 00:02:27.247
But coral reefs would not be possible without the help of coral algae, a type of plant that

32
00:02:27.327 --> 00:02:28.148
lives with corals.

33
00:02:28.708 --> 00:02:34.132
Coral algae produce the phosphates and carbon that the hard corals use to make limestone.

34
00:02:35.833 --> 00:02:39.215
The limestone gives the algae a perfect home, safe from most predators.

35
00:02:39.936 --> 00:02:43.638
In return, the algae help the corals to speed up their production of limestone.

36
00:02:44.179 --> 00:02:49.502
Coral algae, just like any other plant, need sunlight to live, and so coral reefs are almost always

37
00:02:49.582 --> 00:02:51.964
fifty meters or less from the surface of the ocean.

38
00:02:54.292 --> 00:02:57.314
The algae and the corals both contribute to building the coral reef.

39
00:02:57.854 --> 00:03:02.737
When two different types of life live together and help one another, we say they have a symbiotic

40
00:03:02.777 --> 00:03:07.939
relationship. Working together, coral polyps and coral algae create a coral reef.

41
00:03:10.241 --> 00:03:15.063
But the coral reef is more than just a collection of corals and algae; thousands of species of

42
00:03:15.203 --> 00:03:17.324
animals and plants make coral reefs their home.

43
00:03:18.005 --> 00:03:23.848
Some scientists estimate that over 25 percent of all ocean life lives in or near a coral reef.

44
00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:29.022
Many other animals that live near coral reefs also enjoy symbiotic relationships.

45
00:03:31.183 --> 00:03:35.064
There are anemones that will sting, paralyze, and eat any fish that comes nearby.

46
00:03:35.585 --> 00:03:38.226
But these anemones make an exception for the clown fish.

47
00:03:38.866 --> 00:03:43.968
Clown fish help keep the tentacles of the anemones clean, while the anemones provide the clown fish with

48
00:03:44.028 --> 00:03:45.449
a safe place to live and breed.

49
00:03:46.169 --> 00:03:47.330
They also share meals.

50
00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:52.672
Both animals eat fish, and each enjoys the leftovers that the other creature leaves behind.

51
00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:58.241
The coral reef is also home to both hunter and prey.

52
00:03:58.901 --> 00:04:04.023
The parrot fish can take huge bites out of the coral reef, digesting both corals and algae.

53
00:04:05.043 --> 00:04:06.784
The fish spits out the limestone.

54
00:04:07.524 --> 00:04:10.965
Perhaps the oddest predator of the reef is the crown-of-thorns starfish.

55
00:04:12.066 --> 00:04:13.526
It is strange and dangerous.

56
00:04:14.747 --> 00:04:18.388
It slowly moves over the coral reef digesting everything in its path.

57
00:04:20.764 --> 00:04:26.967
Unlike most starfish, it has more than 5 limbs (typically between 12 and 20), and is usually 25

58
00:04:27.007 --> 00:04:30.868
to 30 centimeters wide, but some can grow up to 80 centimeters.

59
00:04:31.789 --> 00:04:36.411
It is bright red and has spines that can inject a poison that would make an unfortunate diver

60
00:04:36.491 --> 00:04:37.311
sick for days.

61
00:04:38.171 --> 00:04:40.672
Some fish use the coral reef to hide from predators.

62
00:04:41.172 --> 00:04:43.533
But predators hide inside the coral reef as well.

63
00:04:45.194 --> 00:04:49.356
The moray eel will suddenly leap out from its hiding place to eat a passing fish.

64
00:04:50.312 --> 00:04:52.254
The octopus, too, can hide in the reef.

65
00:04:52.874 --> 00:04:56.898
It changes color like a chameleon, so that it looks like the surrounding coral reef.

66
00:04:57.498 --> 00:05:01.642
It can squeeze itself into almost any opening and wait for prey to approach.

67
00:05:02.622 --> 00:05:07.687
A crab might be crawling near the reef, only to be captured by the octopus' eight tentacles and

68
00:05:07.807 --> 00:05:08.527
quickly eaten.

69
00:05:10.289 --> 00:05:12.791
After taking many photographs, John surfaced.

70
00:05:13.772 --> 00:05:18.375
Every photograph of a coral reef is valuable, for there may come a time when coral reefs are

71
00:05:18.435 --> 00:05:25.118
extinct. Coral polyps and coral algae are sensitive to temperature, and global warming is heating the ocean, making

72
00:05:25.158 --> 00:05:26.458
it harder for reefs to grow.

73
00:05:29.039 --> 00:05:31.760
Some divers chip off pieces of the reefs to take home.

74
00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:37.261
Some people poison the water near the reefs in order to collect the beautiful fish and sell them

75
00:05:37.301 --> 00:05:38.102
for aquariums.

76
00:05:38.802 --> 00:05:42.083
The poison only paralyzes the fish, but it kills the corals.

77
00:05:43.103 --> 00:05:46.304
Even worse, some fishermen use dynamite near coral reefs.

78
00:05:47.084 --> 00:05:51.968
The fish are killed and float to the surface, while huge parts of the coral reef are damaged.

79
00:05:53.169 --> 00:05:57.093
Many coral reefs are shrinking; they are being damaged faster than they can grow.

80
00:05:58.074 --> 00:06:02.177
John hopes that more people learn to appreciate the beauty and importance of coral reefs.

81
00:06:02.878 --> 00:06:08.342
Perhaps then, he thinks, more people will work to save the reefs, and future generations will always be

82
00:06:08.422 --> 00:06:12.265
able to visit the marvelous underwater world of the ocean's coral reefs.
